  "question": {
    "text": "If the events $\\mathrm{A}$ and $\\mathrm{B}$ are mutually exclusive events such that $\\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{A})=\\frac{3 x+1}{3}$ and $\\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{B})=\\frac{1-x}{4}$, then the set of possible values of $x$ lies in the interval :",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "vGNKU",
      "text": "\n$[0,1]$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "09e9K",
      "text": "\n$\\left[\\frac{1}{3}, \\frac{2}{3}\\right]$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "aDGGJ",
      "text": "\n$\\left[-\\frac{1}{3}, \\frac{5}{9}\\right]$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "qhhCW",
      "text": "\n$\\left[-\\frac{7}{9}, \\frac{4}{9}\\right]$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Since events A and B are mutually exclusive\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\therefore \\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{A})+\\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{B})=1 \\\\\n&\\Rightarrow \\frac{3 x+1}{3}+\\frac{1-x}{4}=1 \\\\\n&\\Rightarrow 12 x+4+3-3 x=12 \\\\\n&\\Rightarrow 9 x=5 \\Rightarrow x=\\frac{5}{9} \\\\\n&\\therefore x \\in\\left[-\\frac{1}{3}, \\frac{5}{9}\\right]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
    "image": null
  },
